        </surface>
      </collision>
      <!-- LANDING LEGS (new): four thin vertical skids at the cardinal
           points (x/y=+/-0.13, one each front/back/left/right), clear of
           the lidar's 0.03m radius and clear of the diagonal arms (which
           sit near x/y=+/-0.17 at 45 degrees off-axis at this z, so the
           cardinal directions fall right between them).
           WHY: the lidar's collision cylinder (above) is centered at
           z=-0.07 with radius 0.03, so its lowest point is z=-0.09 —
           lower than the base_link box's own underside (z=-0.05) and
           lower than anything else on the airframe. With nothing lower,
           the drone's first (and only) ground contact on touchdown was
           the lidar housing itself: a small, off-axis, low-friction
           contact point well forward of the vehicle's center means any
           residual horizontal velocity or slight tilt at touchdown makes
           it rock/tip/skid on that one point instead of settling flat —
           exactly the "unstable at the start" symptom. These four legs'
           tips sit at z=-0.20, 0.11m below the lidar's lowest point, so
           they take first (and all) ground contact. Four points (vs. the
           original two) fully constrain both tilt axes instead of just
           one, so the vehicle can't rock front-to-back the way two
           parallel side skids still could. Mass deliberately not added
           to the link's <inertial> block above: 4 legs at ~0.01kg each
           is <4% of the 1.271kg airframe, the same order of negligible
           mass already folded into that inertial figure for the camera/
           IMU/arms without being separately accounted for. -->
      <collision name='base_footprint_landing_leg_left_collision'>
        <pose>0 0.13 -0.125 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
